# Code standards

## English only

All code artefacts are written in **English**, with no exceptions: comments,
dartdoc, identifiers, commit messages, test names, error and log messages,
and documentation files.

This holds even when the conversation is in German. Chat replies may be German;
anything written to a file may not.

Before finishing a change, check the diff for German. The words that have slipped
through before are ordinary connectives — `ohne`, `wie`, `vor`, `nach`, `kann`,
`gewinnt`, `wird`, `nicht` — not technical terms, so a scan for domain vocabulary
will miss them.

Non-English **test data** is fine and sometimes required (unicode fixtures such
as `'ünïcödé'`). Keep it obviously data, never prose.

## DRY

Two copies of the same logic is one too many. Extract the shared part.

The concrete failure mode in this codebase is *parallel grammars*: the same
parsing or formatting rule implemented once per nesting level or per type. When
adding an accessor or a parse path, look for the routine that already does it.

Duplication that exists only because a type differs belongs behind a generic or
a shared helper, not behind copy-paste.

## Clean code

- One responsibility per function. If the name needs "and", split it.
- Name things for what they mean, not how they work. `_parseInlineEntries`,
  not `_loop2`.
- Guard clauses over nesting. Return early.
- No magic literals. Separators, prefixes and limits belong in `Constants`.
- Dead code, commented-out code and unused parameters are deleted, not kept.
- A comment explains a constraint the code cannot state itself. It never
  restates the next line, and it never records who changed what or why a patch
  is correct.
- Prefer making an illegal state unrepresentable over validating it later.
  Validate in the constructor, not at every call site.

## Enforcement

`dart analyze` and `dart format --set-exit-if-changed` must both be clean before
a change is described. Neither catches language or duplication, so those stay a
review concern.
